An aggregation pipeline is an array of stage documents:

```javascript
db.collection.aggregate([
  { stage1 },
  { stage2 },
  { stage3 }
])
```

A simple example is:

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      status: "completed"
    }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$customerId",
      totalSpent: {
        $sum: "$total"
      },
      orders: {
        $sum: 1
      }
    }
  },
  {
    $sort: {
      totalSpent: -1
    }
  }
])
```

The pipeline operates as follows:

A regular query is appropriate for straightforward retrieval:

```javascript
db.products.find({
  category: "Electronics"
})
```

Aggregation is more appropriate when the result requires calculations or transformations:

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      category: "Electronics"
    }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$category",
      averagePrice: {
        $avg: "$price"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

Use aggregation for questions such as:

`$match` filters documents.

Its syntax is similar to the filter used by `find()`:

```javascript
{
  $match: {
    field: condition
  }
}
```

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      category: "Electronics"
    }
  }
])
```

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      price: {
        $gte: NumberDecimal("50.00"),
        $lt: NumberDecimal("500.00")
      },
      stock: {
        $gt: 0
      }
    }
  }
])
```

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      category: "Electronics",
      active: true,
      stock: {
        $gt: 0
      }
    }
  }
])
```

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      orderedAt: {
        $gte: ISODate("2026-09-01T00:00:00Z"),
        $lt: ISODate("2026-10-01T00:00:00Z")
      }
    }
  }
])
```

When possible, place `$match` near the start of the pipeline:

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      status: "Completed"
    }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$customerId",
      totalSpent: {
        $sum: "$total"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

This reduces the number of documents processed by later stages. An early `$match` may also benefit from an appropriate index.

`$group` combines documents using a grouping key and calculates aggregated values.

General syntax:

```javascript
{
  $group: {
    _id: <grouping-expression>,
    resultField: {
      <accumulator>: <expression>
    }
  }
}
```

The `_id` field defines the grouping key.

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$category",
      productCount: {
        $sum: 1
      }
    }
  }
])
```

Possible result:

```javascript
[
  {
    _id: "Electronics",
    productCount: 2
  },
  {
    _id: "Books",
    productCount: 1
  }
]
```

`$sum: 1` adds one for every document in the group.

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$category",
      averagePrice: {
        $avg: "$price"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$category",

      productCount: {
        $sum: 1
      },

      averagePrice: {
        $avg: "$price"
      },

      lowestPrice: {
        $min: "$price"
      },

      highestPrice: {
        $max: "$price"
      },

      totalInventory: {
        $sum: "$stock"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

Use `_id: null` when the entire input should form one group:

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: null,
      orderCount: {
        $sum: 1
      },
      totalRevenue: {
        $sum: "$total"
      },
      averageOrderValue: {
        $avg: "$total"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: {
        customer: "$customerId",
        status: "$status"
      },
      orderCount: {
        $sum: 1
      },
      totalAmount: {
        $sum: "$total"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

Each unique combination of `status` and `customerId` creates a separate group.

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$category",
      products: {
        $push: "$name"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$status",
      customers: {
        $addToSet: "$customerId"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

To find the most expensive product in each category:

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  {
    $sort: {
      category: 1,
      price: -1
    }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$category",
      mostExpensiveProduct: {
        $first: "$name"
      },
      highestPrice: {
        $first: "$price"
      }
    }
  }
])
```

The preceding `$sort` determines which document is considered first.

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $project: {
      _id: 0,
      orderId: "$_id",
      customer: "$customerId",
      placedOn: "$orderDate",
      amount: "$total",
      status: 1
    }
  }
])
```

This changes only the pipeline result. It does not rename fields in stored documents.

